#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/id #uuid "ec1aaa71-efe4-50fd-8552-bcc98abbd3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-218" :question/problem "Each side of a square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the square decreasing when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](images/a0/a06eb0963dc6d01e45acb9349e5232ba70a11c98c99ee63c2ec0dae3d748f5ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} .\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min},$ so we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = -2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(-2)\\cdot 2(10)=-40{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square decreases at a rate of $40\\,\\text{cm}^2/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/id #uuid "8780fe5c-94c5-5ac6-b6f6-6e86df29b416" :question/math-academy-id "e-219" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $20 \\text{ cm}^2\\text{/s}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds." :question/worked-solution "This situation is different. Here, the *area* is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/f4/f4125cebd7553bb8b106a8baff39a6e4a120b9d9e5cdf2c4c9c0a9a676558897.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\text{ cm[math]^2[/math]/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-20 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220" :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221" :question/problem "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad524f3-747b-543d-877c-19d0bc18ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371592} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d8801c-f0ce-5902-a2aa-2522226312ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371593} {:answer/id #uuid "5f70dac8-847a-5a3c-afa9-5b5ad69de949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371594} {:answer/id #uuid "2c00a4c0-f033-55e1-9d1b-40d157b2fcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371595} {:answer/id #uuid "e6aeaa69-cb2d-572c-ad4b-0228aa3f284a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d8801c-f0ce-5902-a2aa-2522226312ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371593} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59fb865-44ed-5cc4-bf4d-8ff775b26354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "332ede8a-b7d8-56ae-b05d-98854e11eb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115930" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $56{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square decreasing when the sides are $8\\text{ m}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/1c/1cb323f4c38bb4fc13ffc81f47544424872480b42f033032da47c04d097e8696.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-56\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-56 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{28}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $8\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=8.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{28}{8}=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{7}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf51f91-0a6d-569d-8c03-c177edaa1dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398139} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ee6ea-b061-51dc-b501-839195b28399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398140} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4f5765-990d-5307-bcda-feb606bb929b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398141} {:answer/id #uuid "58cdd8af-ecc0-5a53-9201-682ba5b6106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398142} {:answer/id #uuid "d97728d8-7183-59a2-bbab-433924b0cf9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58cdd8af-ecc0-5a53-9201-682ba5b6106b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398142} :answer-field/id #uuid "015670c7-de29-5d58-b9b4-055602c56ffa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c40d4d8-93cd-5fd9-9c9b-8b9eaffb8fe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-116051" :question/problem "![](images/30/30b101fa9812c49237ab72c0c4aebd1666df6de80367eb2ee59b33fe0e422d87.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $3\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-3\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-12\\text{ m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $12\\text{ m}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a61a3e76-4936-5976-abf0-b9b485c4434d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Area Given the Rate of Change of the Side Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a528fa82-0f47-5560-a7f0-a89d1c2b2092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062186} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b68727-7dba-5c37-92d1-64fc343a613c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30m^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062187} {:answer/id #uuid "234f62c4-63b2-5469-bd31-698419004d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20m^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062188} {:answer/id #uuid "6924f190-5ca2-51b2-b6fa-7bf8c91a0649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40m^{2}/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062189} {:answer/id #uuid "6a085e2e-5ee8-5c7b-8f03-c4b35261d43e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062190}] :answer-field/id #uuid "653606cc-002e-51b5-a80e-ba0f2d8215f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062185}] :question/id #uuid "82bcc198-7907-5fda-ad7c-ebdd316cd930" :question/math-academy-id "q-2770" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2m/s$. At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10m$ long?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1659900c-f6c4-5880-9d2a-4ee68c390432" :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Rate of Change of a Diagonal Length to Another Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ffdd0c-16fb-5f50-b5ba-1cb07f13ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372542} :answer-field/id #uuid "6262049f-56fe-5753-8d3a-24cb4987048f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d06153ec-06de-5e08-a5e0-36bc3ef9e7e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-314495" :question/problem "The sides (in feet) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to seven times the rate of increase of the diagonals. What is the length $h$ of the diagonal of the square at that moment?\n\n$h=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$, so replacing $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 7\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 7 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have that the length of the diagonals of the square is $7\\,\\text{ ft}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030b95f8-281c-522a-9c71-ee2a31868eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372901} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6ddcc7-548b-528c-b8e3-9ff38fb06abf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "175199e1-96a5-594b-b670-66eb928d5ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344507" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $30{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a4/a42b7ac5c9f5870d256b6474c9b95730d32184ba22ca80235c76ffe0ff346f31.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\text{ }{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-30 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{15}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1297eee-ae31-559a-8063-29c28074e2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372907} :answer-field/id #uuid "240a125a-7965-503b-98eb-0c6543b7dbff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d743fe47-5489-54f0-9f74-3b3ae11c1658" :question/math-academy-id "q-344614" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f440744e4dd65883ae2ae564595491b36c1371a553c2052d2923f8f617caaf2.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually reduces the size of a square opening by shortening its diagonals at a rate of $2.2\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening decreasing when each diagonal measures $10\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ be the length of the diagonals and $A$ be the area of the square. Using the Pythagorean theorem, the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.2\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2.2.\n$$\n\nWhen the diagonal length is $h=10,$ substituting into the expression above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=(-2.2)(10)=-22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square opening is decreasing at a rate of $22{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2edbb40a-1d59-5c75-bcc0-93584ec9c030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Rate of Change of a Side Length Given the Rate of Change of the Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8407c1-3175-5166-ab8e-c0f6c6813aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062924} {:answer/id #uuid "e1308e12-f501-5153-88f2-76e216435b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062925} {:answer/id #uuid "0456556b-527c-530f-bae6-45f5a5491b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062926} {:answer/id #uuid "368933d9-de3c-5973-9020-320c9eb737b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062927} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbe0d2f-31b8-54e9-b7b0-4f96a8068d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062928}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1e13f342-74ba-5f29-a5e3-5849701e2a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062923}] :question/id #uuid "6d6bf115-1a99-51dd-8a30-6ca9b66d4cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48339" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10in^{2}/hour$. If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{dx}{dt}$, where $t$ is the time in hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2af960-841b-56a9-bc7c-099bf82e0fe8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Rate of Change of Area Given a Rate of Change of a Diagonal: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c33f2db-ee69-5899-b254-c9920d0cc08c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398857} {:answer/id #uuid "07f94e7c-eaf0-50a5-82b2-936289c3244e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398858} {:answer/id #uuid "0b0dea4f-92e3-5582-a8c3-1096bf841a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398859} {:answer/id #uuid "df4e741b-904e-553c-b625-b7472668b725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398860} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5bdcf3-a541-5f6f-8726-df468ddf9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c5bdcf3-a541-5f6f-8726-df468ddf9f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398861} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8c5ef9-0c37-5adf-839c-99af73ffe32d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51c2bb08-d00d-5a09-b26c-2e5b5f8107fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48372" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f636c252da8e886c1f4bf9f0d87387339fce91e5a72f6275e6b24bba787d288.png)\n\nAnna is an environmentalist and is studying the impact of deforestation in a pine forest located in a square-shaped region. Anna discovers that the area of ​​the deforested region is decreasing at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month}.$ At what rate are the region's diagonals decreasing if the diagonals are $20\\text{ km}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $0.4{\\text{ km}}^{2}/\\text{month},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=20.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{0.4}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.02\\,\\text{km}\\text{/month}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the region are decreasing at a rate of  $0.02\\text{ km}/\\text{month}.$"}]}
